# Design

Read `docs/DESIGN.md` before changing anything visual. Encode every number in
`src/lib/design.ts` in the same change. Markdown that is not a token is a rule
nobody follows.

## Never

- A literal hex value, `fontSize`, `fontFamily`, `letterSpacing`, or raw pixel
  gap in a component. Everything comes from `src/lib/design.ts`.
- `StyleSheet.create` at module scope with colours in it. Colours change when the
  scheme flips, so styles come from `useThemedStyles(factory)`.
- Centred type on a page. Headings, paragraphs, empty states, and the wordmark
  start at the left padding. A button's label and icons are centred in the
  capsule. A glyph-only control stays centred in its hit target, and a
  segment's label stays centred in its segment. The content column is still
  centred in a wide window.
- A font family, `fontWeight`, or a second `useFonts` call in a component. The
  interface is Neue Haas Grotesk, through `type`. `face.text` below 20pt,
  `face.display` at 20 and above, in regular, medium, and bold. The file is
  the weight. One `useFonts` call, in `_layout`, loads those six files. The
  wordmark is Display bold, the same cut as a screen title.
- A cut outside `face` — no thin, light, black, or italic, and no `fontWeight`
  written in a component. A weight on one of these files synthesises a fake
  bold on Android. iOS is the exception inside `cut`: it needs the weight or
  it serves Roman.
- `textTransform: 'uppercase'`, or a string literal written in caps. Copy we
  write is sentence case; only proper nouns take a capital. The wordmark is
  written `GRAHAM`. The card's plate is the other exception, capitals with the
  price sheared, because this face has no italic. Dealer data elsewhere
  (brand, model, reference) is printed as imported and never re-cased.
- A glyph from outside `MaterialCommunityIcons`, or a hand-picked size for one.
  Brand marks named below are the exception, and so are the commissioned
  drawings: `assets/filter.svg` at `size.icon`, tinted `text` or `brass`;
  `assets/message.svg` and `assets/comment.svg` at `size.iconSmall`, tinted
  to the control they sit on; `assets/watch.svg` at `size.tabIcon` on the Buy
  tab, tinted to the bar's glyph colour.
  Always the outline cut, `size.icon` or `size.iconSmall`. A selected control
  changes colour. It does not swap to a filled glyph.
- An icon-only control without an `accessibilityLabel` naming what it does.
- `save` or `danger` colour used for anything but saving and passing. A price is
  `money`, because a price in `save` looks like a watch you kept.
- A check after a name, except a dealer in a list of dealers. That mark is
  `VerifiedMark`: gold-filled decagram, bold white check, spoken with the name as
  verified. The deck's title wears none, and so does a person. A check on a
  watch row is still a promise the row did not make.
- A header count assembled from rows already in hand. `listing_counts` counts
  in the database with the deck's predicate; the deck is one pile deep and its
  length is not a total.
- A capsule on anything you do not press. `radius.full` is for a control with a
  label — a button, a chip, a segment, a door on sign-in. Thumbnails take `sm`,
  fields and tiles `md`, the swipe card `lg`. A round photograph is a control
  that is not one.
- A favicon service without `fallback=false`, or a dealer mark that survives a
  failed load. A generic globe beside a shop's name is an invented mark; a miss
  draws nothing. `faviconUrl` is the one place a host is turned into a URL.
- `contentFit="contain"` on a card or a detail hero. Those fill with `cover`.
  A list row is 5/7 and uses `contain`, so the case stays recognisable. The
  full-screen viewer also keeps the whole photograph, and a dealer's mark,
  which is a logo and not a photograph.
- `borderWidth` on four sides of a card, tile, panel, or notice. Depth there
  stays in `surface` / `surfaceSunken` steps. Text fields, selects, and
  community action pills (vote and comment) use `field` plus `fieldChrome()` —
  white (or lifted in dark) with a `border` hairline, not `fill`. A hairline on
  *one* edge is still a separator. `size.rule` is for selection marks, not field
  outlines (`fieldOutline`). The follow capsule keeps a `text` stroke on the page
  colour.
- Any background behind an icon-only control. The glyph is the control; it gets
  a `HIT_TARGET` box of empty space and colour for state, never a block.
- A shadow or elevation in place of the outline you just removed. Depth is the
  colour step between `background`, `surface`, `fill`, and `surfaceSunken`.
  The lone exception is the swipe card: `raised` plus `shadow.card`, because
  white on warm white has no step left and a card in the hand is an object.
  A second caller for `shadow` is a design change, not a style choice.
- `raised` on anything but the swipe card. It is the tone of an object on the
  page. A button is `accent`, not `raised`.
- A card photograph narrower than the card. The height cap does not change
  the width. The plate is the full width, and `cover` centres the watch.
- A third palette. Sign-in ran green as an experiment and does not any more;
  one scheme, keyed off the system, and every screen follows it.
- `text` on type that sits on a `surface` or a `fill`. That is `ink`, and
  `inkMuted` under it. The two are equal in both schemes, so the wrong one
  looks right until the day a palette lifts a container above its page.
- A destructive action that happens on the swipe itself. A row swipe reveals
  the button; the tap is what does it. Nothing in the app undoes a removal.
  The revealed button is a capsule at `inset.compactX` by `inset.compactY`.
- A row inside `SwipeToRemove` whose press handlers skip `useSwipeGuard`. The
  end of a drag fires a click on web, and that click opens the row.
- A line of text teaching a gesture. If the gesture needs the sentence, use the
  gesture the phone already taught.
- A number on a photograph to say how many there are. The roll is bars along
  the top edge, on `scrimTop`, and the count goes in the paging controls'
  `accessibilityValue`.
- The launcher mark in the app, or a tagline anywhere but under the wordmark
  on sign-in. On web, guests land on the deck with the sign-in sheet already
  open over it, once per page load, and no **Log in** in the header; after
  `GUEST_SWIPE_BUDGET` judgments it opens again. On iOS, sign-in is the first screen and that header control does not
  exist. The panel is the wordmark, one `editorial` sentence in `text`, and the
  doors — never a second headline or a card around the doors. The doors name
  the mode: `Create account with` or `Log in with`. No heading does. A header
  holds a title or that screen's own control, a count, and controls. `Screen`
  has no slot for a mark.
- The name Graham on Buy, in capitals or otherwise. Buy's header is the search
  bar, with the count as the subtitle. Sign-in is the one `Wordmark`: the
  letters `GRAHAM` in `type.wordmark`.
- A second line of header type laid under `Screen`'s title. That is its
  `subtitle`, which keeps the header's controls on the title's line.
- A glyph beside a word that stands for the word rather than naming the same
  thing literally. Outside the tab bar and the brass check after a dealer in
  a list, a glyph belongs to a control.
- A second type size under a card's photograph, or muted type there. About
  this watch and Condition are `title` on `text`. The description and the
  condition labels are `label`. A condition value is `labelStrong`. Weight is
  the only difference among the rows.
- A condition block or box-and-papers rows on the card scroll. Condition stays
  in dealer data and the listing editor, not under the photograph on the card.
  The year stays in the name. No specification table on the card.
- A follow or message control on a shop that is not `dealerSocial` in
  `src/lib/design.ts` and `socialButton.ts`. Same shape everywhere: `radius.lg`,
  not `radius.full`.
- A word under a tab glyph, or a tab glyph that is not `MaterialCommunityIcons`
  outline at `size.tabIcon`, except Buy (`assets/watch.svg` at that size) and
  Profile (the photograph). The current tab does not fill its glyph in.
- Scheme colours in the tab bar. It is dark under a pale app in both schemes:
  `bar`, `onBar`, `onBarActive`, and nothing else. `brass` is selection on the
  page, not on the floor: the current tab is white against glyphs at 55% white.
- A tab's count drawn anywhere but half on the glyph's top-right corner, or
  drawn in one colour for both states. It is `space.xs` out on each side, and
  it inverts on the tab you are on — `bar` numerals on `onBarActive` idle, the
  reverse active — because white on a white glyph is one shape, and a ring to
  separate them is an outline.
- A count fetched inside `TabBar`. A screen declares it with `tabBarBadge`
  from a feature hook, and the badge folds into the tab's spoken label.
- A Community tag in a size of its own. The price chip and the intent tag are
  one shape — `caption`, `space.sm` on the sides, flush above and below — and
  only the fill differs.
- A filter row that scrolls sideways. Community's All-and-intents row splits
  its width equally so every choice is on screen.
- The dealer follow list on Community. On desktop web it sits under Filters
  on the Buy rail, at `dealerRail`.
- A ring, outline, or second fill a shade away to show state. Selection is
  `brass`: as a fill with a `background` label, or as the glyph colour. Two
  strokes are allowed. The swipe commit on `SwipeCard` is a gesture in flight,
  drawn on the card's frame so it survives the scroll underneath it. The
  Profile tab's photograph, when that tab is on, is a `size.rule` stroke in
  the bar colour, because a photo has no outline glyph.
- A second control in the view switch, or a glyph in it that names the view you
  are already in. `ViewToggle` is one button showing the destination, and the
  turn-and-fade between the two glyphs is what says a press did anything.
  On the desktop rail the switch is `DeckDisplayButton` instead: the count sits
  where the other tools' glyph does, the label is Display, and a press steps
  it by one, wrapping after six. One per row is the card deck, more is the
  grid. Never a toggle beside a column picker.
- A view swap that cuts. The deck's two views enter on `FadeIn`, which also
  covers `layout.card` giving way to `layout.grid` on a wide screen. Community's
  modes do the same.
- An icon swapped on the frame its state changes. A glyph that becomes another
  glyph goes through `GlyphTurn`, a quarter turn and a cross-fade at
  `motion.fast`. The view toggle and the follow capsule both do.
- A turn, rise, or step on mount. A control that mounts already on shows its
  on state and nothing moves. Initialise the shared value from the prop, then
  animate on change.
- Motion that answers neither "where did that go" nor "did that take". No
  springs, no toasts, no pulse on an idle mark. The moments that do answer are
  listed under "What moves, and why" in `docs/DESIGN.md`; add there before
  adding anywhere else.
- A haptic outside `src/lib/haptics.ts`. `tick` for a threshold or a flip,
  `impact` for a commit, and no third.
- A sent message that waits for the server to appear. The row goes into the
  thread's cache in `onMutate`, marked `PENDING_ID`, and the refetch replaces
  it.
- More than one watch on a grid row for Search, the home grid (`ListingTile`:
  `layout.gridColumnsPhone` on a phone, and on a wide window
  `layout.gridColumns` / `gridColumnsWeb` unless the desktop rail's Display
  control picks up to `gridColumnsMax`), and nowhere else. On desktop web a tile
  stops at `layout.deckGridTileCap`, so two per row does not stretch into a poster.
  Tiles are a square photograph alone
  on the page (`contain`, `radius.md`). Under it, the title and the price share
  a two-line band, the specs are one line, and the shop name wraps across the
  tile. No `surface` wraps the block. The wishlist and a shop use `ListingRow`:
  photograph on the left at `gridScanWidthShare` of the row (cap `gridPhoto`;
  wishlist and any scan row), `gridPhoto` in a shop, details on the right. The
  row or tile does not pass or save — that happens on the listing it opens.
  Search is a wall of square photographs, three across, described in
  `docs/DESIGN.md`.
- `surfaceSunken` on something sitting directly on the page. It is darker than
  the page in the dark scheme and disappears there; it only works inside a
  container.
- A badge around data. A box around a name makes it look like a control, so a
  dealer's name on a card is type and colour, not a box.
- `brass` on a photograph. It is mixed for a warm page and muddies over a
  scrim; the roll's current bar is `onOverlayAccent`. The deck plate is white
  type on `scrim` only.
- Type laid straight on a photograph. It goes in `PhotoCaption`, on `scrim`.
  Half the watches are shot on white and white type vanishes on them.
- The year in a specification line. It belongs to the name — `formatTitle`
  gives "Datejust, 1968", `formatSpecs` gives what the name did not say.
- A sheared price on the deck plate. The price is `Tag` `tone="money"` `size="plate"`
  beside the year with a middot between them, and `fullscreen` at the end of
  that line. Two tight rows: name, then price, year, and expand.
- A capsule padded by less than half its height. The rounded end covers the
  label, and iOS clips it. Horizontal padding is `size.controlMin / 2`.
- A step count on an onboarding slide. The heading is the question. The
  footer stays at the bottom, and above the keyboard while it is open.
- An onboarding field that waits for a tap before the keyboard opens. The
  first field is focused when the slide settles, and the footer does not
  dismiss the keyboard.
- A birthday typed into three boxes, or set with a slider. It is three wheels
  (month, day, year) that snap to one value. A thumb cannot sit on one year.
- A ghost Back on an onboarding slide, or an arrow on the heading's line.
  Back is an arrow on its own row above the heading, at the left, with no fill.
  The first slide and the age-blocked slide have no arrow.
- **Skip for now** on an onboarding question. Continue stays off until a row
  is chosen.
- A `CheckRow` label in the regular cut. It is `labelStrong`, the medium cut.
- A filled field for a name or a username on onboarding. That answer is a
  line at `type.title`, with no fill. The shop claim stays `Field`. The
  username's @ is drawn beside the line, not typed, on the same baseline, with
  `space.md` before the letters. The collector line under
  it is `body` in `textMuted`.
- An onboarding slide that starts flush under the notch inset. The question
  sits `space.xl` lower. The footer stays at the bottom.
- `lineHeight` on a single-line field, or `inset.controlY` as its vertical
  padding. Take the face and size from `type.body`. `paddingBottom` is
  `space.xs` and the top padding is 0, so the letters sit in the middle of
  `size.controlMin`. A multiline field keeps `type.body` and `inset.controlY`.
- A filled button that is not `accent` with `onAccent` type, centred. That
  pair is the inverse of the page: near-black on a light page, light on a
  dark one. `ghost` is the button with no fill. `solid` is the same pair.
  **View on dealer site** is the hollow capsule in `docs/DESIGN.md`: no fill,
  a `text` stroke, `size.follow` tall, `link-variant` at `size.iconSmall`.
- A redrawn Instagram mark beside a shop's handle. The mark is the white
  glyph in `assets/instagram.png`, tinted to `text`, at `size.icon`. The
  handle is the label, in `body`. The site beside it uses `web` at the same
  size, and the domain is the label. Both rows are at least `HIT_TARGET` tall.
- A recolored Google G, or a glyph in its place. The door is an outline: page
  fill, a `text` stroke, label in `text`. The mark is `assets/google-g.png`,
  the four-color G, at `size.providerMark`. Apple's native button is black on
  a light page and white on a dark one. The email door uses `email-outline`
  in `text`.
- An elliptical arc in a bundled SVG. expo-image's iOS decoder distorts packed
  arc flags, and the failure shows up on one platform only.
- `Dimensions.get('window')` at module scope. It never updates on a browser
  resize. Use `useWindowDimensions` for the viewport, `onLayout` for an element.
- A screen, card, or text column with no maximum width. Widths come from
  `layout`; `Screen` applies one by default.
- `Platform.OS` used to ask how much room there is. That is a width question,
  so branch on width. Reserve `Platform.OS` for capability.
- `showsVerticalScrollIndicator={false}` on any scroll view but the swipe
  card's and the birthday wheels. Web bars are already gone document-wide in
  `hideScrollbars`, so the prop only reaches the native indicator, which
  elsewhere is transient and the platform's. The card is a held object and its
  right edge is beside the photograph. A bar on a wheel sits between the
  columns.
- A colour or width token for a scrollbar. There is no bar to size.
- A hardcoded number for a notch, Dynamic Island, or home indicator. Insets
  come from `react-native-safe-area-context`.
- A guess at where a navigator put something. The save flight measures the
  Wishlist bookmark with `measureInWindow` rather than dividing the window by the
  tab count, and degrades to no animation until that measurement arrives.
- A `tabBar*` styling option in the tabs layout. The bar is `TabBar`; the
  layout declares routes, titles, icons, counts, and `tabBarPosition`, and
  nothing about how any of it looks.
- A custom bar that changes where a tab goes. Emit `tabPress`, respect a
  prevented default, then navigate — the navigator's own order.

## Always

- Spread a style from `type` rather than setting size, family, or tracking by hand.
- Use `space`, `inset`, `stack`, and `radius`. Page gutter (`inset.screen`)
  never pads a field or compact option. Sheets use `stack.group`, pages use
  `stack.section`. If the design needs a value that is not in the scale, change
  the scale deliberately and write down why.
- Check both schemes. Dark mode is a warm charcoal, not an inversion.
- Keep tappable areas at `HIT_TARGET` or give them `hitSlop`, on every platform.
- Check a narrow phone and a wide browser window. Resize the window: layout
  that reads a stale size looks right until it moves.
- On desktop web, a left rail and the column beside it share one top edge. Put
  `paddingTop` on both or on neither — never an inset on only the rail or only
  the main column to "line up" with something inside the neighbor. If the feed
  or grid is flush, the rail is flush too.
- Desktop Buy has the same uncapped page in both views (`width="grid"`,
  `desktopBleed`), so the tool rail does not move when Display
  switches views. That rail is `dealerRail`: the dealer list sits under
  Filters. A button-only rail stays `deckToolRail`. The card stage alone caps
  at `cardWeb`, centred beside the rail. Tab bodies with no title use
  `columnDesktopFlush` (`space.lg` under the marketplace header).
- Pass `inTabs` to `Screen` from a screen inside `(tabs)`, and only from there.
  A bottom tab bar owns the bottom inset; a wide left rail does not.
- Pass `inSheet` to `Screen` from a route presented as `modal`, and only from
  there. The sheet's card already starts below the status bar.
- Pad safe-area insets from `useSafeAreaInsets`. `SafeAreaView` reads its own
  position on screen and reports nothing for the top inside a `fullScreenModal`.
- Give every gesture a keyboard or pointer equivalent. A mouse has no thumb.
- Use `CheckRow` for multi-select lists. Selection is a trailing checkbox on
  `motion.fast`, not a brass left rule. A form field with a handful of mutually
  exclusive answers is one dropdown, the way the composer names selling, buying,
  or trading — not a list of rows.
- Render everything below a watch's price from `ListingDetails`. The deck card
  and the listing route are two frames on one block, and the frame passes in its
  own buttons.
- Scroll inside a swipe card with React Native's `ScrollView`. Gesture
  Handler's takes the whole touch on web and the card stops moving. Keep
  `activeOffsetX` and `failOffsetY` on the pan so the two axes stay separate.

## When the rules do not cover it

Decide, build it, and add the rule to `docs/DESIGN.md` and `src/lib/design.ts`
in the same change. An undocumented one-off becomes the thing the next screen
copies.
